\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Overview of the LongPhase-TO somatic haplotype phasing framework. (a) Haplotype-aware recalibration refines initial somatic variant calls from ClairS-TO or DeepSomatic-TO by integrating PoNs and haplotype linkage information, distinguishing true somatic variants (orange) from sequencing artifacts (yellow) and germline variants (blue). (b) Chromosome-scale LOH detection identifies large-scale allelic imbalances by analyzing CNV/BFB intervals and heterozygosity ratios to distinguish LOH from non-LOH regions. (c) Somatic phasing assigns somatic variants to their originating germline haplotypes (HP1, HP2) and reconstructs derived somatic haplotypes (HP1-1, HP2-1), spanning LOH regions. (d) Phasing-informed copy-number fitting estimates tumor DNA fraction and ploidy from read depth and haplotype-resolved allelic balance, with LOH segments constraining the DNA fraction. (e) Haplotagged reads at a somatic SNV in HCC1395\_HKU: reads are partitioned into the germline haplotypes HP1 and HP2 and the somatic haplotype HP2-1, which carries the somatic allele and descends from HP2. (f) Haplotagging across an LOH boundary in the same dataset: within the LOH segment (left), where HP1 is lost, somatic reads remain tagged HP2-1 rather than collapsing into HP2, and both parental haplotypes are recovered in the adjacent non-LOH region (right).}}{12}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{Figure_1.pdf}{{1}{12}{Overview of the LongPhase-TO somatic haplotype phasing framework. (a) Haplotype-aware recalibration refines initial somatic variant calls from ClairS-TO or DeepSomatic-TO by integrating PoNs and haplotype linkage information, distinguishing true somatic variants (orange) from sequencing artifacts (yellow) and germline variants (blue). (b) Chromosome-scale LOH detection identifies large-scale allelic imbalances by analyzing CNV/BFB intervals and heterozygosity ratios to distinguish LOH from non-LOH regions. (c) Somatic phasing assigns somatic variants to their originating germline haplotypes (HP1, HP2) and reconstructs derived somatic haplotypes (HP1-1, HP2-1), spanning LOH regions. (d) Phasing-informed copy-number fitting estimates tumor DNA fraction and ploidy from read depth and haplotype-resolved allelic balance, with LOH segments constraining the DNA fraction. (e) Haplotagged reads at a somatic SNV in HCC1395\_HKU: reads are partitioned into the germline haplotypes HP1 and HP2 and the somatic haplotype HP2-1, which carries the somatic allele and descends from HP2. (f) Haplotagging across an LOH boundary in the same dataset: within the LOH segment (left), where HP1 is lost, somatic reads remain tagged HP2-1 rather than collapsing into HP2, and both parental haplotypes are recovered in the adjacent non-LOH region (right)}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Comparison of LOH-aware phasing and LOH calling. (a) Haplotype block N50 for three germline-based phasing tools (HapCUT2, WhatsHap, LongPhase) and the LOH- and somatic-aware phaser LongPhase-TO across eight datasets from six cancer cell lines. (b) Total length of phased haplotypes for the same methods and datasets. (c) LOH N50 of ASCAT in tumor-normal (T+N) and tumor-only (TO) modes and of LongPhase-TO using somatic variants from ClairS-TO or DeepSomatic-TO. (d) LOH burden (fraction of the autosomal genome with LOH) of ASCAT (T+N), ASCAT (TO), and LongPhase-TO (ClairS-TO and DeepSomatic-TO). (e) Orthogonal comparison in HCC1395, the only cell line with an available independent LOH profile: chromosome ideograms show reference cytobands (top), LOH segments reported by CytoScan HD microarray (middle, purple) and LOH segments inferred by LongPhase-TO (bottom, blue).}}{13}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{Figure_2.pdf}{{2}{13}{Comparison of LOH-aware phasing and LOH calling. (a) Haplotype block N50 for three germline-based phasing tools (HapCUT2, WhatsHap, LongPhase) and the LOH- and somatic-aware phaser LongPhase-TO across eight datasets from six cancer cell lines. (b) Total length of phased haplotypes for the same methods and datasets. (c) LOH N50 of ASCAT in tumor-normal (T+N) and tumor-only (TO) modes and of LongPhase-TO using somatic variants from ClairS-TO or DeepSomatic-TO. (d) LOH burden (fraction of the autosomal genome with LOH) of ASCAT (T+N), ASCAT (TO), and LongPhase-TO (ClairS-TO and DeepSomatic-TO). (e) Orthogonal comparison in HCC1395, the only cell line with an available independent LOH profile: chromosome ideograms show reference cytobands (top), LOH segments reported by CytoScan HD microarray (middle, purple) and LOH segments inferred by LongPhase-TO (bottom, blue)}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces LongPhase-TO improves somatic SNV and indel calling on nanopore and PacBio HiFi data. (a) SNV (top) and indel (bottom) F1-score of ClairS-TO and DeepSomatic-TO at each tumor DNA fraction for the eight nanopore cell-line datasets. (b) The same for the six PacBio HiFi cell-line datasets. Solid colored lines show each caller with LongPhase-TO recalibration, and gray dashed lines show the caller alone (open circles for ClairS-TO and open squares for DeepSomatic-TO). The triplet-graph coefficients and thresholds were fitted on nanopore data and applied to PacBio HiFi data without refitting. DeepSomatic-TO with LongPhase-TO uses the ClairS-TO normal panels. Precision and recall of each dataset are shown in Supplementary Figures~\ref {fig:recal-ont-snv}, \ref {fig:recal-ont-indel}, \ref {fig:pacbio-snv-recalibration} and~\ref {fig:pacbio-indel-recalibration}.}}{14}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{Figure_3.pdf}{{3}{14}{LongPhase-TO improves somatic SNV and indel calling on nanopore and PacBio HiFi data. (a) SNV (top) and indel (bottom) F1-score of ClairS-TO and DeepSomatic-TO at each tumor DNA fraction for the eight nanopore cell-line datasets. (b) The same for the six PacBio HiFi cell-line datasets. Solid colored lines show each caller with LongPhase-TO recalibration, and gray dashed lines show the caller alone (open circles for ClairS-TO and open squares for DeepSomatic-TO). The triplet-graph coefficients and thresholds were fitted on nanopore data and applied to PacBio HiFi data without refitting. DeepSomatic-TO with LongPhase-TO uses the ClairS-TO normal panels. Precision and recall of each dataset are shown in Supplementary Figures~\ref {fig:recal-ont-snv}, \ref {fig:recal-ont-indel}, \ref {fig:pacbio-snv-recalibration} and~\ref {fig:pacbio-indel-recalibration}}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Phasing-informed estimation of tumor DNA fraction and ploidy. (a) Absolute error of the estimated tumor DNA fraction at each expected fraction for ASCAT (T+N), ASCAT (TO), PURPLE (TO) and LongPhase-TO. Points are the eight nanopore and six PacBio HiFi cell-line dilution series and the five HG008 nanopore mixtures (open symbols). ASCAT was not run on HG008. Bars mark the median, and the numbers above the panel give the mean absolute error over all mixtures. ASCAT and PURPLE purities were converted to DNA fraction with each tool's fitted ploidy. Supplementary Figure~\ref {fig:pacbio-tdf} shows the estimates for each platform. (b) Estimated ploidy minus reference ploidy for PURPLE (TO) and LongPhase-TO, for each cell line across all mixtures of both platforms. The reference is the published ploidy of each cell line and the NIST mean total copy number (1.47) for HG008. The band marks $\pm 0.2$. (c) Total copy number of the pure HG008 tumor along chromosomes 1 to 22 for PURPLE (TO) (top) and LongPhase-TO (bottom), drawn over the NIST v0.6 benchmark (gray). Chromosome 10 has no truth. Triangles mark segments with a copy number above 4.5. Right, percentage of the evaluated genome whose estimated total copy number lies within a given tolerance of the NIST value, for the pure tumor (thick lines) and the four mixtures at expected DNA fractions of 0.2 to 0.8 (thin lines). Points mark a tolerance of 0.25 in the pure tumor. LongPhase-TO values are from the DeepSomatic-TO configuration with the ClairS-TO normal panels.}}{15}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{Figure_4.pdf}{{4}{15}{Phasing-informed estimation of tumor DNA fraction and ploidy. (a) Absolute error of the estimated tumor DNA fraction at each expected fraction for ASCAT (T+N), ASCAT (TO), PURPLE (TO) and LongPhase-TO. Points are the eight nanopore and six PacBio HiFi cell-line dilution series and the five HG008 nanopore mixtures (open symbols). ASCAT was not run on HG008. Bars mark the median, and the numbers above the panel give the mean absolute error over all mixtures. ASCAT and PURPLE purities were converted to DNA fraction with each tool's fitted ploidy. Supplementary Figure~\ref {fig:pacbio-tdf} shows the estimates for each platform. (b) Estimated ploidy minus reference ploidy for PURPLE (TO) and LongPhase-TO, for each cell line across all mixtures of both platforms. The reference is the published ploidy of each cell line and the NIST mean total copy number (1.47) for HG008. The band marks $\pm 0.2$. (c) Total copy number of the pure HG008 tumor along chromosomes 1 to 22 for PURPLE (TO) (top) and LongPhase-TO (bottom), drawn over the NIST v0.6 benchmark (gray). Chromosome 10 has no truth. Triangles mark segments with a copy number above 4.5. Right, percentage of the evaluated genome whose estimated total copy number lies within a given tolerance of the NIST value, for the pure tumor (thick lines) and the four mixtures at expected DNA fractions of 0.2 to 0.8 (thin lines). Points mark a tolerance of 0.25 in the pure tumor. LongPhase-TO values are from the DeepSomatic-TO configuration with the ClairS-TO normal panels}{figure.caption.5}{}}
